<h2>A woman without shelter, an application without a date</h2>
<p>The petitioner&rsquo;s circumstances were stark. She had suffered burn injuries over more than eighty per cent of her body in an incident of April 2025. She alleged that on 7 April 2026 she was assaulted and shut out of the shared household in Badarpur. She then filed a complaint under Section 12 of the DV Act read with Sections 18, 20 and 22, together with an application for a residence order under Section 19, registered as CT Case No. 1183/2026 before the Mahila Court at Saket.</p>
<p>The Magistrate had already recorded a prima facie finding of a domestic relationship and a shared household, and had issued notice. But the residence application itself remained undecided — while the petitioner and her two minor children remained without shelter. It was that gap between a prima facie finding and an operative order that brought her to the High Court.</p>

<h2>What the High Court directed</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. A fixed timeline.</strong> The JMFC-01 (Mahila Court), South East District, Saket, is to decide the Section 19 application within four weeks — including the prayer for interim residence or alternative accommodation under Section 23.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. A pleading schedule.</strong> Replies within two weeks; rejoinder within three days thereafter. The schedule leaves no room for the familiar cycle of adjournments sought to outlast an urgent application.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. A default rule.</strong> If the respondents fail to file within time, the Magistrate may proceed to decide the application on the available record. Delay by one side no longer stalls the relief.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Interim protection.</strong> Until the application is disposed of, the respondents are restrained from creating third-party rights in the shared household — so the property cannot be sold out from under the proceedings.</div>
</div>

<h2>The sixty-day mandate, taken seriously</h2>
<p>The legal anchor of the order is Section 12(5) of the DV Act, which requires the Magistrate to endeavour to dispose of a DV application within sixty days of the first hearing. In practice, that timeline is honoured more in the breach; interim applications for residence and maintenance routinely pend for months. The High Court&rsquo;s order treats the provision as what it is — a legislative judgment that reliefs under this statute lose their meaning if they come late. A residence order granted after a year of homelessness protects very little.</p>
<div class="note">The High Court decided nothing on merits. It expressly left the Section 19 application open for the Magistrate to decide either way. The writ jurisdiction was used only to compel a timely decision and to preserve the property in the meanwhile.</div>

<h2>What practitioners can take from the order</h2>
<p>First, where an interim application under the DV Act is pending beyond the statutory horizon, a focused petition seeking expedition — rather than a merits challenge — is often the fastest and least risky route. Second, a prayer for restraint on alienation of the shared household should accompany any such petition; without it, a favourable residence order can be rendered academic by a transfer executed mid-proceedings. Third, a structured pleading schedule with a default clause is worth seeking in terms, because it converts a general direction to &ldquo;expedite&rdquo; into something a trial court can actually enforce against a non-cooperating party.</p>
<p>For respondents, the order is an equally clear signal: once a prima facie domestic relationship is recorded, delay is not a defence strategy the system will indulge, and dealings with the shared household during the pendency of a residence application invite restraint orders of exactly this kind.</p>
